"A railroad diagram expresses a grammar rule as traversable paths through tokens, alternatives, optional branches, and repetition.\n\nUse when: Teach the accepted syntax of a command or language construct. Review a compact grammar rule with non-specialist readers.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns semantic behavior or ambiguous parsing beyond the shown grammar.\n\nVisual grammar: A start-to-end path encodes a valid token sequence. Branch tracks encode alternatives or omission. Returning tracks encode repetition, with terminals distinguished from nonterminals.\n\nBuild: Select one named grammar production and its authoritative version. Lay out the required token sequence on a main track. Add alternative, optional, and repetition paths with explicit return direction. Reserve generous track spacing so tokens and branch bends cannot collide.\n\nCheck: Every accepted path corresponds to an intended production. Repetition minimums and separators are unambiguous. Referenced nonterminals have definitions elsewhere. Tracks never run through token text or create misleading intersections.\n\nPitfalls: Using a loop that accidentally permits an empty or trailing separator. Combining many productions into a dense, unreadable track network.\n\nExample: A command accepts IMPORT followed by a file name and an optional FORMAT clause, with a single clear exit.\n\nAlternatives: Abstract syntax tree, API resource diagram."
